Sure! Below is a simple example of a 4-bit adder implemented in Verilog. This adder takes two 4-bit binary numbers as input and produces a 4-bit sum and a carry-out bit.

```verilog
module adder_4bit (
    input [3:0] a,    // 4-bit input a
    input [3:0] b,    // 4-bit input b
    input cin,        // carry-in
    output [3:0] sum, // 4-bit sum output
    output cout      // carry-out
);

    // Internal wires to hold intermediate carry values
    wire [3:0] carry;

    // Full adder for each bit
    full_adder fa0 (.a(a[0]), .b(b[0]), .cin(cin), .sum(sum[0]), .cout(carry[0]));
    full_adder fa1 (.a(a[1]), .b(b[1]), .cin(carry[0]), .sum(sum[1]), .cout(carry[1]));
    full_adder fa2 (.a(a[2]), .b(b[2]), .cin(carry[1]), .sum(sum[2]), .cout(carry[2]));
    full_adder fa3 (.a(a[3]), .b(b[3]), .cin(carry[2]), .sum(sum[3]), .cout(cout));

endmodule

// Full adder module
module full_adder (
    input a,          // input a
    input b,          // input b
    input cin,        // carry-in
    output sum,       // sum output
    output cout       // carry-out
);

    // Logic for sum and carry-out
    assign sum = a ^ b ^ cin;
    assign cout = (a & b) | (b & cin) | (a & cin);

endmodule
```

### Explanation:
- **adder_4bit**: This is the top-level module that takes two 4-bit inputs (`a` and `b`), a carry-in (`cin`), and produces a 4-bit sum (`sum`) and a carry-out (`cout`).
- **full_adder**: This is a sub-module that performs the addition of two bits along with a carry-in, producing a sum and a carry-out.
- **Wires**: The `carry` wires are used to pass the carry-out from one full adder to the carry-in of the next full adder.

This code can be synthesized to create a 4-bit adder circuit in an FPGA or ASIC.
